Triton AMD backend: never fold a scf.while's own exit condition to a constant.

Applies to: triton-lang/triton 0a6e13e6e31f18fa75a17ac72dee0dd1bcb507b8
(release/3.9.x). Independent of the other patches in this directory.

WHY
---
A triton kernel with a `while` loop nested inside a `for` loop whose trip count
is exactly 1 hangs the GPU on launch. The card stays at full clock, the kernel
never returns, and dmesg shows `ring gfx timeout` followed by a BACO reset.
Killing the host process does not release the GPU.

vLLM's `_topk_topp_kernel` (vllm/v1/sample/ops/topk_topp_triton.py) has this
shape. Each program grid-strides over rows with
`for row_id in tl.range(pid, BATCH_SIZE, num_programs)` and runs a bounded search
`while` loop per row. At BATCH_SIZE=1 with one program, the outer loop runs
once. A trivial 5-iteration `while` with no data dependency hangs the same way
inside the same `tl.range` loop. So the bug is the loop structure, not the
kernel's arithmetic.

The pass that breaks it is `TritonAMDFoldTrueCmpI` (`FoldTrueCmpIOp` in
third_party/amd/lib/Analysis/RangeAnalysis.cpp). It replaces an `arith.cmpi` with
a constant whenever `TritonIntegerRangeAnalysis` proves the comparison statically
true or false. With `MLIR_ENABLE_DUMP=1`, the while's condition block reads
`%1 = arith.cmpi eq, %done, %c0_i32; scf.condition(%1)` before this pass and
`scf.condition(%true)` after it. The range analysis of triton 3.8 (675c59878)
bounds its fixed point over loop-carried values by one number: the combined trip
count of a loop and all its enclosing loops. With an outer trip count of 1, the
analysis stops before the while's back-edge value from `scf.yield` is joined in.
It sees
the exit-condition operand only at its initial value and "proves" the comparison
constant. `%true` means the loop never exits. `%false` means its body, including
every store in it, never runs. Nothing in the pass is arch-specific, so the
mechanism is not gfx803-specific. gfx803 is only where it was seen on hardware.

This commit contains upstream 236bc7af2 ("[AMD] Fix under-approximated
loop-carried ranges in range analysis"), which reworks that trip-count budget.
Its regression tests cover nested `scf.for` loops and a dynamic one-trip loop,
not a `scf.while` inside a `scf.for` with a trip count of 1. Whether the analysis
still folds this case wrongly at this commit is open. The repro below, run once
with this patch and once without it, answers that.

WHAT
----
`FoldTrueCmpIOp::matchAndRewrite` refuses to fold a `cmpi` that feeds the
`scf.condition` of its own enclosing `scf.while`, whatever the range analysis
says. In this pass, that is the one place where a wrong answer is a hang or a
skipped loop body instead of a missed optimization. Every other `cmpi` stays
eligible. The guard sits in the pattern and not in the range analysis.
Other passes use the analysis too, buffer-op conversion among them, and a change
there reaches all of them.

VERIFICATION
------------
NOT YET RE-VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE on triton 3.9. The last hardware run used
triton 3.8 (675c59878) on an RX 470. Three cases completed with correct results,
and the GPU returned to idle after each:
- the minimal repro
- the full search loop at grid 1 to 32 and batch 1 to 8
- vLLM's `apply_top_k_top_p_triton(logits, k, p)` at batch 8 and vocab 151936
Without the patch, every one of them hung.

Repro to run on this commit, on gfx803, once with this patch and once without:
1. A kernel whose body is `for row in tl.range(pid, BATCH_SIZE, num_programs):`
   around a 5-iteration `while` loop with no data dependency, which stores its
   final counter. Run it at BATCH_SIZE=1 and grid=(1,). It must return 5.
2. vLLM's `apply_top_k_top_p_triton(logits, k, p)` at batch 8 and vocab 151936.
   It must return, and the GPU must go back to idle.
Watch dmesg for `ring gfx timeout` during both runs.

diff --git a/third_party/amd/lib/Analysis/RangeAnalysis.cpp b/third_party/amd/lib/Analysis/RangeAnalysis.cpp
index 7c6547af2..3402230b3 100644
--- a/third_party/amd/lib/Analysis/RangeAnalysis.cpp
+++ b/third_party/amd/lib/Analysis/RangeAnalysis.cpp
@@ -842,8 +842,27 @@ struct FoldTrueCmpIOp : OpRewritePattern<arith::CmpIOp> {
   FoldTrueCmpIOp(MLIRContext *context, DataFlowSolver *solver)
       : OpRewritePattern(context), solver(solver) {};
 
+  // Folding a scf.while's own exit condition to a constant turns a wrong range
+  // into a hang (true: the loop never exits) or a skipped body (false), not a
+  // missed optimization. The range analysis has produced exactly that wrong
+  // range for a while nested in a for-loop whose trip count is 1, so this one
+  // cmpi is never folded, whatever the analysis claims. Every other cmpi still
+  // is.
+  static bool isOwnWhileConditionOperand(arith::CmpIOp cmpOp) {
+    for (Operation *user : cmpOp.getResult().getUsers()) {
+      auto conditionOp = dyn_cast<scf::ConditionOp>(user);
+      if (!conditionOp || conditionOp.getCondition() != cmpOp.getResult())
+        continue;
+      if (isa_and_nonnull<scf::WhileOp>(conditionOp->getParentOp()))
+        return true;
+    }
+    return false;
+  }
+
   LogicalResult matchAndRewrite(arith::CmpIOp cmpOp,
                                 PatternRewriter &rewriter) const override {
+    if (isOwnWhileConditionOperand(cmpOp))
+      return failure();
     auto result = evaluateCmpI(*solver, cmpOp);
     if (!result)
       return failure();
